{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' <original-home>/.codex/adversarial_review.md && printf '\\\\n--- source 1196-1335 ---\\\\n' && nl -ba sources/manuscript/main.tex | sed -n '1196,1335p' && printf '\\\\n--- candidate/live prerequisite byte equality ---\\\\n' && sha256sum AristotleCandidates/CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean AristotleCandidates/GeneralLatticeProjectionTower.lean && sha256sum aristotle_responses/P49-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49-gizp5x2z_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean aristotle_responses/P49-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49-gizp5x2z_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/GeneralLatticeProjectionTower.lean && printf '\\\\n--- existing candidate target? ---\\\\n' && test -e AristotleCandidates/FiniteCornerTrace.lean; echo \\\"exists=\"'$?\" && git check-ignore -v aristotle_responses/P49-finite-corner-trace.tar.gz aristotle_responses/P49-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49-gizp5x2z_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/FiniteCornerTrace.lean || true'","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review Protocol\n\nUse this before closing substantive Codex work. The goal is to find defects, not\nto summarize accomplishments.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Identify the artifact types touched: `code`, `math`, `tex`, `computation`,\n   `source-backed`, `workflow`.\n2. Inspect the diff or new artifact against the user's request.\n3. Report findings by severity first. If there are no findings, say so and state\n   the remaining risk.\n\n## Universal Checks\n\n- Did the work answer the actual latest user request?\n- Did it modify unrelated files or broaden scope?\n- Did it overwrite, reformat, or revert pre-existing user changes?\n- Are generated files, caches, logs, plots, PDFs, or build artifacts handled\n  intentionally?\n- Are final claims stronger than the evidence supports?\n- Are verification commands actually relevant to the changed artifact?\n\n## Code Checks\n\n- Does the changed behavior match the stated interface and edge cases?\n- Are tests missing for a changed behavior, bug fix, parser, numerical routine,\n  CLI command, or user-facing workflow?\n- Are errors handled where inputs, files, network calls, or external tools can\n  fail?\n- Is there hidden global state, path sensitivity, nondeterminism, race risk, or\n  stale cache behavior?\n- Did dependency, config, or environment changes alter unrelated workflows?\n\n## Math Checks\n\n- Are all assumptions, domains, signs, conventions, normalizations, and boundary\n  conditions stated before use?\n- Are definitions used consistently throughout the derivation?\n- Is any implication actually reversible, or was a one-way statement used both\n  ways?\n- Are there division-by-zero, invertibility, regularity, convergence, branch-cut,\n  gauge, or boundary-term assumptions?\n- Do limiting cases, dimensions/units, symmetries, and known special cases agree?\n- Are symbolic substitutions justified rather than smuggling in the result?\n- Are claims labeled correctly as proof, derivation, check, conjecture, or\n  numerical evidence?\n\n## TeX And Source-Backed Checks\n\n- Does the document compile if compilation was expected?\n- Are labels, references, citations, bibliography entries, and figure paths valid?\n- Are source-backed claims tied to exact locators when required?\n- Is quoted or paraphrased material clearly separated from the agent's synthesis?\n- Does the exposition define terms before using them in load-bearing statements?\n\n## Computation Checks\n\n- Is the computation reproducible from a recorded command?\n- Are inputs, random seeds, versions, tolerances, and units documented when they\n  affect the result?\n- Are outputs interpreted correctly and not over-promoted beyond the computation?\n- Were independent sanity checks used where feasible: small cases, limiting\n  cases, dimensional analysis, symbolic identities, or alternate algorithms?\n\n## Closeout Template\n\nUse this structure internally, then surface the concise version to the user:\n\n```text\nFindings:\n- [severity] file/path:line - issue and consequence.\n\nChecks run:\n- command -> result.\n\nResidual risk:\n- what was not checked, or why the artifact is not a proof/complete validation.\n\nCleanup:\n- remaining dirty/untracked files and whether they are intentional.\n```\n\n--- source 1196-1335 ---\n  1196\tbounded by $\\max(\\norm{a},\\norm{b})< \\infty$.\n  1197\tBy \\cite[I.3.2.1]{Blackadar} multiplication is jointly strongly continuous on bounded sets, so\n  1198\t\\begin{equation}\n  1199\t  p_Nap_Nbp_N\n  1200\t  \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}}\n  1201\t  qaqbq.\n  1202\t\\end{equation}\n  1203\tThe same holds with $a$ and $b$ interchanged.  Taking the limit\n  1204\tof~\\eqref{eq:local_state} and using~\\eqref{eq:local_traciality}\n  1205\tproves~\\eqref{eq:local_trace}.\n  1206\t\\end{proof}\n  1207\tNow that we have traciality on simple operators, we can study it on \n  1208\t\n  1209\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:cstar_trace}\n  1210\tFor every $a,b\\in\\cU_\\Gamma$,\n  1211\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:Cstar-trace}\n  1212\t  \\tau_q\\bigl((qaq)(qbq)\\bigr)\n  1213\t  =\n  1214\t  \\tau_q\\bigl((qbq)(qaq)\\bigr).\n  1215\t\\end{equation}\n  1216\t\\end{lemma}\n  1217\t\n  1218\t\\begin{proof}\n  1219\tSince $\\cU_\\G$ is the norm closure of $\\cU_{\\G,\\mathrm{fin}}$, we can choose sequences $a_k,b_k\\in\\cU_{\\Gamma,\\mathrm{fin}}$ such that\n  1220\t\\begin{equation}\n  1221\t  a_k \\xrightarrow{\\norm{\\cdot}} a,\n  1222\t  \\qquad\n  1223\t  b_k \\xrightarrow{\\norm{\\cdot}} b.\n  1224\t\\end{equation}\n  1225\tConsider the operators\n  1226\t\\begin{equation}\n  1227\t  X_k:=qa_kq,\n  1228\t  \\quad\n  1229\t  Y_k:=qb_kq,\n  1230\t  \\quad\n  1231\t  X:=qaq,\n  1232\t  \\quad\n  1233\t  Y:=qbq.\n  1234\t\\end{equation}\n  1235\tSince $\\norm{q} \\leq 1$ and the operator norm is submultiplicative as a\n  1236\t$C^\\ast$-norm\n  1237\t\\cite[II.1.1.1]{Blackadar} the map $x \\mapsto q x q$ satisfies\n  1238\t\\begin{equation}\n  1239\t    \\norm{q x q} \\leq \\norm{q} \\norm{x} \\norm{q} \\leq \\norm{x},\n  1240\t\\end{equation}\n  1241\ti.e. it is contractive. Therefore we have \n  1242\t\\begin{equation}\n  1243\t  X_k \\xrightarrow{\\norm{\\cdot}} X,\n  1244\t  \\qquad\n  1245\t  Y_k \\xrightarrow{\\norm{\\cdot}} Y.\n  1246\t\\end{equation}\n  1247\tBy submultiplicativity of the norm, we also have that \n  1248\t\\begin{equation}\n  1249\t    X_kY_k \\xrightarrow{\\norm{\\cdot}} XY,\n  1250\t    \\qquad\n  1251\t    Y_kX_k \\xrightarrow{\\norm{\\cdot}} YX.\n  1252\t\\end{equation}\n  1253\tSince $\\tau_q$ is a bounded linear function, it is continuous wrt. the operator\n  1254\tnorm. Therefore\n  1255\t\\begin{equation}\n  1256\t  \\tau_q(XY)\n  1257\t  =\\lim_k\\tau_q(X_kY_k)\n  1258\t  =\\lim_k\\tau_q(Y_kX_k)\n  1259\t  =\\tau_q(YX).\n  1260\t\\end{equation}\n  1261\t\\end{proof}\n  1262\tNow that we know that the state is tracial on the compression of the\n  1263\t$C^\\ast$-algebra, we want to extend this result to the full von Neumann\n  1264\talgebra $M_\\G$.\n  1265\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:normal-trace}\n  1266\tThe state $\\tau_q$ defines a normal tracial state on $q\\cM_\\Gamma q$.\n  1267\t\\end{proposition}\n  1268\t\n  1269\t\\begin{proof}\n  1270\tBy Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists} $q \\in M_\\G$ and \\cite[I.9.1.4]{Blackadar} implies\n  1271\tthat $qM_\\G q$ is a von\n  1272\tNeumann algebra considered on the Hilbert space $q \\cH_\\w$.\n  1273\tTo proof the assertion, we need to first proof, that $q \\cU_\\G q$ is strongly\n  1274\tdense in $q M_\\G q$, to approximate the functional on it.\n  1275\tFor that, let $x \\in M_\\G$. By Kaplanskys Density theorem\n  1276\t\\cite[I.9.1.3]{Blackadar}, there exist a uniformly bounded net $(x_i)_{i\\in I}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ with $x_i \\xrightarrow{\\sigma\\text{-}\\mathrm{s}} x$ and hence\n  1277\tstrongly. By the same argument as in the previous lemma then\n  1278\t\\begin{equation}\n  1279\t    q x_i q \\xrightarrow{\\sigma\\text{-}\\mathrm{s}} q x q,\n  1280\t\\end{equation}\n  1281\tstrongly which proves that $q \\cU_\\G q$ is strongly dense in $q M_\\G q$. The\n  1282\tsame argument implies that for every $a,b \\in M_\\G$ there exist uniformly bounded nets $(a_i)_{i\\in I},(b_j)_{j\\in J}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ such that\n  1283\t\\begin{equation}\n  1284\t   q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q,\n  1285\t   \\qquad\n  1286\t   q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q.\n  1287\t\\end{equation}\n  1288\tTo argue for the convergence of the product, we can introduce a joint index $k$\\footnote{For example $k\n  1289\t    = (i,j)$ so that $a_{(i,j)} = a_i, b_{(i,j)} = b_j$ and $(i',j') \\preceq (i,j)\n  1290\t    \\Leftrightarrow i' \\preceq i \\land j' \\preceq j$},\n  1291\tso that by the same argument as in the proof of Lemma \\ref{lem:finite-trace}\n  1292\t\\begin{equation}\n  1293\t    q a_i q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q b q,\n  1294\t    \\qquad\n  1295\t    q b_j q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q a q.\n  1296\t\\end{equation}s\n  1297\tSince the nets $q a_n q, q\n  1298\tb_n q$ are uniformly bounded and the product on uniformly bounded sets is\n  1299\tstrongly continuous \\LS{REF},\n  1300\t\\begin{equation}\n  1301\t    \\begin{aligned}\n  1302\t        \\t_q( (q a q ) (q b q)) &= \\bra{\\W}  (q a q ) (q b q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n  1303\t                                & = \\lim_{k \\rightarrow \\infty}\\bra{\\W}  (q a_k\n  1304\t                                q ) (q b_k q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n  1305\t                                & = \\lim_{k \\rightarrow \\infty}\\bra{\\W}  (q b_k\n  1306\t                                q ) (q a_k q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n  1307\t                                &= \\bra{\\W}  (q b q ) (q a q)\\ket{\\W}\n  1308\t    \\end{aligned}\n  1309\t\\end{equation}\n  1310\twhere we used strong convergence of the nets in the second line which proves\n  1311\tcyclicity on $q M_\\G q$. Since $\\t_q$ is a restriction of a vector functional\n  1312\tto $qM_\\G q$ it is normal. Since on $q\\cH_\\w, q$ is the identity, we have that $\\t(1) =\n  1313\t\\bra{\\W} q \\ket{\\W} = \\langle \\W | \\W \\rangle = 1$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists}.\n  1314\tThis implies that $\\t_q$ defines a normal state on the von Neumann algebra $qM_\\G q$. \n  1315\t\\end{proof}\n  1316\t\n  1317\t\\subsection{Faithfulness and finiteness}\n  1318\tNow that we established that the state $\\t_q$ is tracial for $q M_\\G q$, we\n  1319\twant to use it to restrict the full algebra $M_\\G$. The central decomposition\n  1320\tof $M_\\G$ asserts that we can decompose our Hilbert space into an integral\n  1321\t\\begin{equation}\n  1322\t    \\cH_\\w \\simeq \\int_X \\cH_x d\\m(x),\n  1323\t\\end{equation}\n  1324\twhere $X$ is a measurable space equipped with a measure $\\m$, such that for\n  1325\teach measurable set $E \\subset X$ one obtains a projection $z_E$ which is in\n  1326\tthe center of $M_\\G$ such that\n  1327\t\\begin{equation}\n  1328\t    z_E \\cH_\\w \\simeq \\int_E \\cH_x d\\m(x).\n  1329\t\\end{equation}\n  1330\tSimilarly one has a decomposition \n  1331\t\\begin{equation}\n  1332\t    M_\\G \\simeq \\int_X M_x d\\m(x),\n  1333\t\\end{equation}\n  1334\twhere $M_x \\subset \\cB(\\cH_x)$ is a factor almost surely. Our goal is to proof\n  1335\tthat none of the $M_x$ can have type III. To do so, we will proof that every\n\n--- candidate/live prerequisite byte equality ---\nc55831ac16d3dec3d471c25c24159a5c22c79dff1460c586830eadda27da57d8  AristotleCandidates/CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean\n190af007e460f439360a0e195c6458e23f0aa5146009cb7f6b40ece2ea6b2386  AristotleCandidates/GeneralLatticeProjectionTower.lean\nc55831ac16d3dec3d471c25c24159a5c22c79dff1460c586830eadda27da57d8  aristotle_responses/P49-quarantine/aristotle-stabilizer_two_lane_p49-gizp5x2z_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/CenterFiniteProjectionCore.lean\n190af007e460f439360a0e195c6458e23f0aa5